# Universal Link fallback. Users with ok2eat installed: iOS intercepts the
# URL via apple-app-site-association and opens the app directly — they
# never hit this redirect. Users without the app (or on desktop) get
# bounced to the App Store. Status 302 (temporary) so search engines
# don't cache it.
/open  https://apps.apple.com/us/app/ok2eat/id6761730687  302
/open/* https://apps.apple.com/us/app/ok2eat/id6761730687  302

# v1.21 — Public recipe pages. The iOS Share button on the recipe sheet and
# the daily digest's recipe deep-links both point at ok2eat.com/recipes/<slug>.
# iOS users with the app installed hit Universal Links (configured in
# /.well-known/apple-app-site-association) and never reach Netlify. Everyone
# else hits Netlify, where this rewrite turns the pretty URL into the
# query-string form the SPA-style /recipes/index.html reads. Using "rewrite"
# semantics (status 200) instead of redirect (302) so the URL bar stays clean.
# Recipe slug shape is restricted to lowercase alphanumeric + hyphens so this
# can't accidentally intercept /recipes/index.html or future static assets.
/recipes/:slug   /recipes/index.html?id=:slug   200


# scanner that ran without auth, but a failed scan (faded receipt, dark
# photo, odd format) became a poor first impression. Receipt scan still
# exists for logged-in users inside the iOS app + web app — we just don't
# want anonymous visitors using it as the first taste of the product.
# Force-redirect (the trailing ! shadows even if /scan/index.html is still
# on disk) so we can keep the source around for quick reactivation.
/scan      /  302!
/scan/*    /  302!
